<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch07_P06_Ex4_Difference</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>For the exercise above, let \(f(n) = 2n^3 + 3n^2 + n\).</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Expand and simplify \(f(k+1) - f(k)\).</p>
<div>\(f(k+1) - f(k) =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Now assume that \(f(k) = 6m\) for some integer \(m\). Complete the line below. Give your answer in terms of \(k\) and \(m\).</p>
<div>\(f(k+1) = 6 \times \Big(\) [[input:ans2]] \(\Big)\) [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Expand \(f(k+1)\) using \((k+1)^3 = k^3 + 3k^2 + 3k + 1\) and \((k+1)^2 = k^2 + 2k + 1\):</p>
<p>\[f(k+1) = 2(k+1)^3 + 3(k+1)^2 + (k+1) = 2k^3 + 9k^2 + 13k + 6.\]</p>
<p>Subtract \(f(k) = 2k^3 + 3k^2 + k\):</p>
<p>\[f(k+1) - f(k) = 6k^2 + 12k + 6 = 6(k+1)^2.\]</p></li>
<li><p>So \(f(k+1) = f(k) + 6(k+1)^2\). By the induction hypothesis \(f(k) = 6m\), so</p>
<p>\[f(k+1) = 6m + 6(k+1)^2 = 6\left(m + (k+1)^2\right),\]</p>
<p>and \(m + (k+1)^2\) is an integer. So \(f(k+1)\) is divisible by 6, which is the statement for \(n = k+1\).</p></li>
</ol>]]></text></generalfeedback>
